#: ../../../history.rst:5
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:6
msgid ""
"Import your best Leonard Nimoy documentary voice and get ready for the xonsh "
"tutorial on ``history``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:10
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:12
msgid ""
"By default xonsh implements a collection of JSON-formatted history files "
"that can be thought of as having the following structure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:15
msgid ""
"{'env': {...},  # Environment that xonsh was started with\n"
" 'sessionid': str, # UUID4 for the session\n"
" 'ts': [start, stop],  # start and stop timestamps for session [s since "
"epoch]\n"
" 'locked': True,  # boolean for whether the file is in use or not\n"
" 'cmds': [  # array of commands\n"
"    {'inp': str,  # input command\n"
"     'ts': [start, stop],  # timestamps for the command\n"
"     'rtn': int, # command return code\n"
"     'out' str,  # stdout and stderr of command, for subproc commands\n"
"                 # this is only available select OSs. Off by default.\n"
"     },\n"
"    ...\n"
"    ],\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:32
msgid ""
"This rich set of data allows xonsh to do much more advanced inspection and "
"manipulation. The sessionid, locking, and one-file-per-shell ideas allow for "
"there to be multiple instances of xonsh running at the same time without "
"competing and overwriting history constantly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:39
msgid "``history`` command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:40
msgid ""
"All xonsh history inspection and manipulation goes through the top-level "
"``history`` alias or command.  If you run this without an ``action`` "
"argument, it will default to the ``show`` action, see below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:44
msgid "@ history"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:48
msgid ""
"Also note that the history object itself can be accessed through the xonsh "
"built-in variables ``@.history`` and ``__xonsh__.history``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:53
msgid "``show`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:54
msgid ""
"The ``show`` action for the history command mimics what the ``history`` "
"command does in other shells.  Namely, it displays the past inputs along "
"with the index of these inputs. This operates on the current session by "
"default and is the default action for the ``history`` command. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:59
msgid ""
"@ 1 + 1\n"
"2\n"
"@ history show\n"
" 0  1 + 1\n"
"@ history\n"
" 0  1 + 1\n"
" 1  history show"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:70
msgid "History is zero-indexed; this is still Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:72
msgid ""
"The show command can also optionally take as an argument any integer (to "
"just display that history index) or a slice (to display a range of history "
"indices). To display only the even indices from above, you could write:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:76
msgid ""
"@ history show ::2\n"
" 0  1 + 1\n"
" 2  history"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:82
msgid ""
"One can also use many slice/integer arguments to get different portions of "
"history"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:84
msgid ""
"After ``show`` an option that indicates which history to be returned can be "
"used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:86
msgid ""
"``xonsh`` displays the past inputs from all valid json files found in "
"``XONSH_DATA_DIR``. As such, this operates on all past and present xonsh "
"sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:90
msgid "``all`` is an alias for ``xonsh``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:92
msgid ""
"``zsh`` will display all history from the history file specified by the "
"``HISTFILE`` environmental variable in zsh. By default this is ``~/."
"zsh_history``. However, they can also be respectively specified in both ``~/."
"zshrc`` and ``~/.zprofile``. Xonsh will parse these files (rc file first) to "
"check if ``HISTFILE`` has been set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:98
msgid ""
"The ``bash`` action will display all history from the history file specified "
"by the ``HISTFILE`` environmental variable in bash. By default this is ``~/."
"bash_history``. However, they can also be respectively specified in both "
"``~/.bashrc`` and ``~/.bash_profile``. Xonsh will parse these files (rc file "
"first) to check if ``HISTFILE`` has been set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:105
msgid ""
"``show`` also accepts other options for more control over history output, "
"the ``-n`` option is used to enumerate the commands, the ``-t`` option is "
"used to show the timestamps, and more, try out ``history show --help`` for a "
"list of options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:111
msgid "Filtering by date and time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:113
msgid ""
"Use ``--start-time`` and ``--end-time`` to filter history by timestamp. Both "
"accept the format set in ``$XONSH_DATETIME_FORMAT`` (default ``%Y-%m-%d %H:"
"%M``) as well as any ISO-8601 date or datetime:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:117
msgid ""
"@ # all commands from a specific day (across all sessions)\n"
"@ history show all --start-time 2025-07-17 --end-time 2025-07-18\n"
"\n"
"@ # with time (quote the space, or use T as separator)\n"
"@ history show all --start-time \"2025-07-17 14:00\" --end-time \"2025-07-17 "
"18:00\"\n"
"@ history show all --start-time 2025-07-17T14:00 --end-time "
"2025-07-17T18:00\n"
"\n"
"@ # combine with other options\n"
"@ history show all --start-time 2025-07-17 --end-time 2025-07-18 -n -t"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:129
msgid "Times are interpreted in your local timezone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:133
msgid "``id`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:134
msgid ""
"Each xonsh history has its own universally unique ``sessionid``. The ``id`` "
"action is how you display this identified. For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:137
msgid ""
"@ history id\n"
"ace97177-f8dd-4a8d-8a91-a98ffd0b3d17"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:143
msgid "``file`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:144
msgid ""
"Similarly, each xonsh history has its own file associated with it. The "
"``file`` action is how you display the path to this file. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:147
msgid ""
"@ history file\n"
"/home/me/.local/share/xonsh/xonsh-ace97177-f8dd-4a8d-8a91-a98ffd0b3d17.json"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:152
msgid ""
"Note that by these files are stored in ``$XONSH_DATA_DIR`` environment "
"variable. This is, by default, set to the ``xonsh`` dir inside of the free "
"desktop standards ``$XDG_DATA_HOME`` environment variable. See `this page "
"<http://standards.freedesktop.org/basedir-spec/latest/ar01s03.html>`_ for "
"more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:159
msgid "``info`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:160
msgid ""
"The info action combines the ``id`` and ``file`` actions as well as adds "
"some additional information about the current state of the history. By "
"default, this prints a key-value series of lines. However, it can also "
"return a JSON formatted string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:164
msgid ""
"@ history info\n"
"sessionid: ace97177-f8dd-4a8d-8a91-a98ffd0b3d17\n"
"filename: /home/snail/.local/share/xonsh/xonsh-ace97177-f8dd-4a8d-8a91-"
"a98ffd0b3d17.json\n"
"length: 6\n"
"buffersize: 100\n"
"bufferlength: 6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:173
msgid ""
"@ history info --json\n"
"{\"sessionid\": \"ace97177-f8dd-4a8d-8a91-a98ffd0b3d17\",\n"
" \"filename\": \"/home/snail/.local/share/xonsh/xonsh-ace97177-"
"f8dd-4a8d-8a91-a98ffd0b3d17.json\",\n"
" \"length\": 7, \"buffersize\": 100, \"bufferlength\": 7}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:182
msgid "``diff`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:183
msgid ""
"Between any two history files, we can run the ``diff`` action. This does "
"more that a simple line diff that you might generate with the unix ``diff`` "
"command. (If you want a line diff, just use the unix command!) Instead this "
"takes advantage of the fact that we know we have xonsh history files to do a "
"more sophisticated diff on the environment, input, output (if available), "
"and return values.  Of course, the histories inputs should be 'sufficiently "
"similar' if the diff is to be meaningful. However, they don't need to be "
"exactly the same."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:190
msgid ""
"The diff action has one major option, ``-v`` or ``--verbose``. This "
"basically says whether the diff should go into as much detail as possible or "
"only pick out the relevant pieces. Diffing the new and next examples, we see "
"the diff looks like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:194
msgid ""
"@ history diff ~/new.json ~/next.json\n"
"--- /home/snail/new.json (35712b6f-4b15-4ef9-8ce3-b4c781601bc2) [unlocked]\n"
"started: 2015-08-27 15:13:44.873869 stopped: 2015-08-27 15:13:44.918903 "
"runtime: 0:00:00.045034\n"
"+++ /home/snail/next.json (70d7186e-3eb9-4b1c-8f82-45bb8a1b7967) [unlocked]\n"
"started: 2015-08-27 15:15:09.423932 stopped: 2015-08-27 15:15:09.619098 "
"runtime: 0:00:00.195166\n"
"\n"
"Environment\n"
"-----------\n"
"'PATH' is in both, but differs\n"
"- /home/snail/.local/bin:/home/snail/sandbox/bin:/home/snail/miniconda3/bin:/"
"usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/"
"local/games:/home/snail/origen22/code/\n"
"+ /home/snail/.local/bin:/home/snail/sandbox/bin:/home/snail/miniconda3/bin:/"
"home/snail/.local/bin:/home/snail/sandbox/bin:/home/snail/miniconda3/bin:/"
"usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/games:/usr/"
"local/games:/home/snail/origen22/code/:/home/snail/origen22/code/\n"
"\n"
"'SHLVL' is in both, but differs\n"
"- 2\n"
"+ 3\n"
"\n"
"'XONSH_INTERACTIVE' is in both, but differs\n"
"- True\n"
"+ False\n"
"\n"
"These vars are only in 70d7186e-3eb9-4b1c-8f82-45bb8a1b7967: {'OLDPWD'}\n"
"\n"
"Commands\n"
"--------\n"
"cmd #4 in 35712b6f-4b15-4ef9-8ce3-b4c781601bc2 input is the same as\n"
"cmd #4 in 70d7186e-3eb9-4b1c-8f82-45bb8a1b7967, but output differs:\n"
"Outputs differ\n"
"- 2  10\n"
"+ 2  7  10\n"
"\n"
"cmd #5 in 35712b6f-4b15-4ef9-8ce3-b4c781601bc2 input is the same as\n"
"cmd #5 in 70d7186e-3eb9-4b1c-8f82-45bb8a1b7967, but output differs:\n"
"Outputs differ\n"
"- /home/snail/new.json\n"
"+ /home/snail/next.json"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:232
msgid "As can be seen, the diff has three sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:234
msgid ""
"**The header** describes the meta-information about the histories, such as "
"their file names, sessionids, and time stamps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:236
msgid ""
"**The environment** section describes the differences in the environment "
"when the histories were started."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:238
msgid "**The commands** list this differences in the command themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:240
msgid ""
"For the commands, the input sequences are diff'd first, prior to the outputs "
"being compared. In a terminal, this will appear in color, with the first "
"history in red and the second one in green."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:245
msgid "``flush`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:246
msgid ""
"Normally, the history entries are kept in memory and are only saved to disk "
"once the in-memory buffer gets full. This is in order to reduce unnecessary "
"I/O and to keep session history free from noise from other sessions. "
"Sometimes, however, it may be useful to share entries between shell "
"sessions. In such a case, one can use the ``flush`` action to immediately "
"save the session history to disk and make it accessible from other shell "
"sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:254
msgid "``pull`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:255
msgid ""
"Tries to pull the history from parallel sessions and add to the current "
"session."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:257
msgid ""
"For example if there are two parallel terminal windows the run of ``history "
"pull`` command from the second terminal window will get the commands from "
"the first terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:260
msgid ""
"The optional `--session-id` allows you to specify that history should only "
"be pulled from a specific other session. Most useful when using the JSON "
"history backend, as the overhead of an unfiltered `pull` can be "
"significantly higher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:265
msgid "``clear`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:266
msgid ""
"Deletes the history from the current session up until this point. Later "
"commands will still be saved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:270
msgid "``erasedups`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:271
msgid ""
"Removes duplicate commands from history, keeping only the latest occurrence "
"of each command. Works with both JSON and SQLite backends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:274
msgid ""
"@ history erasedups\n"
"Removed 42 duplicate entries (187 total)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:281
msgid "``off`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:282
msgid ""
"Deletes the history from the current session and turns off history saving "
"for the rest of the session. Only session metadata will be saved, not "
"commands or output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:286
msgid "``on`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:287
msgid ""
"Turns history saving back on. Previous commands won't be saved, but future "
"commands will be."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:291
msgid "``gc`` action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:292
msgid ""
"Last, but certainly not least, the ``gc`` action is a manual hook into "
"executing history garbage control. Since history has the potential for a lot "
"of information to be stored, it is necessary to be able to clean out the "
"cache every once in a while."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:297
msgid ""
"Garbage control is launched automatically for every xonsh thread, but runs "
"in the a background thread. The garbage collector only operates on unlocked "
"history files. The action here allows you to manually start a new garbage "
"collector, possibly with different criteria."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:302
msgid ""
"Normally, the garbage collector uses the environment variable "
"``$XONSH_HISTORY_SIZE`` to determine the size and units of what should be "
"allowed to remain on disk. By default, this is ``(8128, 'commands')``. This "
"variable is usually a tuple or list of a number and a string, as seen here.  "
"However, you can also use a string with the same information, e.g. ``'8128 "
"commands'``.  On the command line, though, you just pass in two arguments to "
"the ``--size`` option, a la ``--size 8128 commands``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:309
msgid "The garbage collector accepts four canonical units:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:311
msgid ""
"``'commands'`` is for limiting the number of past commands executed in the"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:312
msgid "history files,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:313
msgid ""
"``'files'`` is for specifying the total number of history files to keep,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:314
msgid ""
"``'s'`` is for the number of seconds in the past that are allowed - which is "
"effectively a timeout of the history files, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:316
msgid ""
"``'b'`` is for the number of bytes that are allowed on the file system for "
"all history files to collectively consume."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:319
msgid ""
"However, other units, aliases, and appropriate conversion functions have "
"been implemented. This makes it easier to garbage collect based on human-"
"friendly values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:322
msgid "**GC Aliases:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:324
msgid ""
"{'commands': ['', 'c', 'cmd', 'cmds', 'command'],\n"
" 'files': ['f'],\n"
" 's': ['sec', 'second', 'seconds', 'm', 'min', 'mins', 'h', 'hr', 'hour', "
"'hours',\n"
"       'd', 'day', 'days', 'mon', 'month', 'months', 'y', 'yr', 'yrs', "
"'year', 'years'],\n"
" 'b': ['byte', 'bytes', 'kb', 'kilobyte', 'kilobytes', 'mb', 'meg', 'megs', "
"'megabyte',\n"
"       'megabytes', 'gb', 'gig', 'gigs', 'gigabyte', 'gigabytes', 'tb', "
"'terabyte',\n"
"       'terabytes']\n"
" }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:335
msgid ""
"So all said and done, if you wanted to remove all history files older than a "
"month, you could run the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:338
msgid "@ history gc --size 1 month"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:344
msgid "History Indexing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:345
msgid ""
"History object (``@.history``, ``__xonsh__.history``) acts like a sequence "
"that can be indexed by int or slice. At the moment only history from the "
"current session can be retrieved. Note that the most recent command is the "
"last item in history."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:350
msgid ""
"Indexing with an **int** returns a single ``HistoryEntry`` object with "
"attributes ``cmd``, ``out``, ``rtn``, ``ts``, and ``cwd``. Indexing with a "
"**slice** returns a list of ``HistoryEntry`` objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:354
msgid ""
"@ echo mkdir with/a/huge/name/\n"
"mkdir with/a/huge/name/\n"
"@ @.history[-1].cmd\n"
"'echo mkdir with/a/huge/name/\\n'\n"
"@ @.history[-1].rtn\n"
"0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:365
msgid "Exciting Technical Detail: Lazy JSON"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:366
msgid ""
"So now you know how to inspect, run, and remove history. But what *is* a "
"history file exactly? While xonsh history files are JSON formatted, and they "
"do have the structure indicated at the top of the page, that isn't their top-"
"level structure.  If you open one up, you'll see a bunch of hocus pocus "
"before you get to anything real."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:371
msgid ""
"Xonsh has implemented a generic indexing system (sizes, offsets, etc)for "
"JSON files that lives inside of the file that it indexes.  This is known as "
"``LazyJSON`` because it allows us to only read in the parts of a file that "
"we need. For garbage collecting based on the number of commands, we can get "
"this information from the index and don't need to read in any of the "
"original data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:377
msgid ""
"The best part about this is that it is totally generic. Feel free to use "
"``xonsh.lib.lazyjson`` yourself for things other than xonsh history! Of "
"course, if you want to read in xonsh history, you should probably use the "
"module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:383
msgid "Exciting Technical Detail: Teeing and Pseudo Terminals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:384
msgid ""
"Xonsh is able to capture all stdout and stderr transparently and "
"responsively. For aliases, Python code, or xonsh code, this isn't a big "
"deal. It is easy to redirect information flowing through ``sys.stdout`` and "
"``sys.stderr``.  For subprocess commands, this is considerably harder. "
"Capturing stdout during the session is disabled by default but can be "
"enabled by setting ``$XONSH_CAPTURE_ALWAYS=True``. Storing stdout to the "
"history backend is disabled by default but can be enabled by setting "
"``$XONSH_STORE_STDOUT=True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:391
msgid ""
"To be able to tee stdout and stderr and still have the terminal responsive, "
"xonsh implements its own teeing pseudo-terminal on top of the Python "
"standard library ``pty`` module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:396
msgid "Sqlite History Backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:398
msgid ""
"Xonsh has a second built-in history backend powered by sqlite (other than "
"the JSON version mentioned all above in this tutorial). It shares the same "
"functionality as the JSON version in most ways, except it currently doesn't "
"support the ``history diff`` action and does not store the output of "
"commands, as the json-backend does. E.g. `@.history[-1].out` will always be "
"`None`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:405
msgid ""
"The Sqlite history backend can provide a speed advantage in loading history "
"into a just-started xonsh session. The JSON history backend may need to read "
"potentially thousands of json files and the sqlite backend only reads one. "
"Note that this does not affect startup time, but the amount of time before "
"all history is available for searching."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:411
msgid ""
"To use sqlite history backend, set ``$XONSH_HISTORY_BACKEND = 'sqlite'`` in "
"your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>`. To switch back to JSON version, remove this "
"line, or set it to ``'json'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:415
msgid ""
"SQLite history backend currently only supports ``commands`` as the unit in "
"``$XONSH_HISTORY_SIZE`` in its garbage collection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../history.rst:418
msgid ""
"If you have `sqlite-web <https://pypi.python.org/pypi/sqlite-web>`_ "
"installed, you can read the history easily with command: ``sqlite_web "
"@$(history file)``."
msgstr ""

